  "worked_solution": "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(1)=3\\sqrt{1}=3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at  $(7,0).$",
